  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nAs of 130 PM Wednesday afternoon, a midlevel ridge axis extends from \neastern TX through upper MO valley. A stout midlevel low is \npositioned off of the Baja with an impressive plume of Pacific \nmoisture streaming northward across the southern Rockies and high \nPlains. Weak WAA from 850-700 mb is resulting in scattered showers \nacross the western half of KS. A few showers may slide into portions \nof Barton and Russell counties this afternoon but rain amounts will \nremain a few hundredths to a tenth. Otherwise, the stretch of \nseasonable temperatures continues with highs topping out in the \n70s/near 80 this afternoon. \n\nTransitioning into tonight and Thursday, the midlevel ridge axis \nwill slowly progress east, expanding the WAA axis eastward and \nbringing rain chances towards the I-135 corridor. Lapse rates will \nsteepen slightly to allow for 300-500 J/kg of MUCAPE to develop. \nBrief heavy rainfall and occasional lightning will be the main \nconcerns with any thunderstorms late tonight into Thursday. \n\nThe rain chances will continue into Friday and even increase late \nFriday into Saturday as a midlevel shortwave trough emerges from the \nsouthwest US and ejects across the central Plains. This should allow \nfor widespread rainfall. Fortunately, lapse rates profiles will \nremain weak and severe thunderstorms are not expected. Beyond \nSaturday, a midlevel ridge axis will slide across the central US \nSunday into Monday as a trough axis comes ashore across the \nsouthwest US. This trough axis will slide across the central Plains \nTuesday into Wednesday, returning rain chances to the area.",
